{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790998541000-architect-floatfix-refusal-ruling.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log; tail -5 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790998541000-architect-floatfix-refusal-ruling.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc | sed -n '315,375p'; rg -n 'reject carrier|coerc|Float64|print' docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790998541000-architect-floatfix-refusal-ruling.claude-fable-5-1.stdout.log; tail -5 /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1790998541000-architect-floatfix-refusal-ruling.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log; nl -ba src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc | sed -n '315,375p'; rg -n 'reject carrier|coerc|Float64|print' docs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-03 03:36:31 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-03 10:36:31 +07\n\n**Verdict: SHIP-WITH-CHANGES** — keep the refusal in the float-fix slice, with these four changes:\n\n1. Drop `:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable` from the error.\n2. Refuse every non-native numeric carrier, not just float64.\n3. Record it in 8.5.5 and in `dao.jing.cbor.md` *Numeric identity*, not in the UCF scalar-arm paragraph.\n4. Pin every op in `carrier-refusing` in the test.\n\nI read the diff, the docs and the test; I ran nothing.\n\n## 1. Ship it\n\nThe refusal is correct and belongs in this slice, for three reasons.\n\n- **This slice creates the hazard.** It admits the carrier at `plain-data?`, `machine-data?` and the `scalar?` gate, so a JS `Float64` object can now reach `+` from a decoded row. Before the slice it could not. Admitting the carrier without guarding arithmetic ships a silent wrong answer: `(+ carrier 1)` on JS goes through `toString` and yields the string `\"21\"`.\n- **It protects \"same address on every host\".** Without it, the JVM continues with `3.0` and JS continues with `\"21\"`, and both lift to valid but different addresses. With it, JS produces no address at all.\n- **It does not make yin.vm profile-aware.** The check references only `dao.jing.cbor`, the substrate `yin.vm.values` already depends on. The Python seam `data/float-value` lives in `yin.vm.data`, and the VM never calls it. The alternative, unwrapping inside `+`, is the choice that would violate \"one representation\": JS would return bare `3`, an integer, where the JVM returns float `3.0`.\n\nThere is also direct precedent. `dao.space.query/host-arithmetic` (`src/cljc/dao/space/query.cljc:897`) already does this. `dao.jing.cbor.md` *Numeric identity* records it as \"reject carrier operands loudly rather than computing garbage … consistent with VM arithmetic remaining outside this migration\". The VM refusal applies that ruling at the point where the VM first meets carriers.\n\n## 2. Changes, recording, asymmetry, coverage\n\n**Change 1: drop the `:yin.k/non-portable` status.** UCF §7.9 is a closed outcome algebra, and `:yin.k/non-portable` is raised by lift with `:yin.k/path`, `:yin.k/kind` and `:yin.k/hint`. `ucf.cljc:231` dispatches on that status in thrown ex-data. A primitive's runtime type refusal is not a lift outcome, and reusing the status risks it being caught and reported as one. Use the shape of the query precedent (`{:fn op, :operand x, :type …}`) or a plain primitive-error ex-data with no `:yin.k/status`.\n\n**Change 2: refuse every numeric carrier.** The current check is `(and (cbor/float64? x) (not (number? x)))`. Decimal and Rational carriers coerce through `+` just as badly on JS. Use the query predicate `(and (cbor/numeric? x) (not (number? x)))`, with one caveat: confirm it does not start refusing BigInt, which C3 ruling 4 routes through `integer-host`. If it would, exclude `big-carrier?`.\n\n- I would also drop the `#?(:cljs … :default f)` gate and let the predicate decide per host, as the query version does. The rule then reads the same everywhere: host-native arithmetic refuses non-native numeric carriers.\n- The Dart Decimal and Rational carriers would then refuse too, which is correct.\n\n**Change 3: where it is recorded.**\n\n- **8.5.5** gets one sentence: generic-yin `+ - * / < > <= >=` refuse the carrier, `= == !=` stay host `=`, and Python reaches float arithmetic only through `data/float-value`.\n- **`dao.jing.cbor.md` *Numeric identity*** gets the extension, next to the query-builtins sentence. That sentence currently says VM arithmetic is \"outside this migration\", which this change makes stale.\n- **The UCF scalar-arm paragraph stays as is.** It governs what lifts, not what primitives compute.\n\n**The asymmetry is acceptable.** A uniform refusal is not implementable, and the two reasons are worth recording.\n\n- On the JVM and Dart, float64 content is a host double, with no carrier to detect. Refusing uniformly would mean refusing all double arithmetic in generic yin.\n- The divergence is not created by the refusal. Generic-yin float arithmetic was already non-portable, since a bare JS `2.0 + 1` is the integer `3`. The refusal turns one silent case into a loud one.\n\n**Change 4: `+`-only coverage is not enough.** Each op is a separate registry entry, wrapped only by set membership, so a typo or rename in `carrier-refusing` silently unwraps one. Extend the test with:\n\n- a `doseq` over all eight ops, with the carrier in each operand position;\n- one variadic call with the carrier third, to exercise the `& more` arm;\n- one positive pin that `(= carrier carrier)` still answers.\n\n**Follow-up, not this slice:** the query builtins route `< > <= >=` through Jing's portable compare, while the VM refuses them. Ordering could later compute portably in the VM. Refusing now is the conservative choice and can be relaxed without breaking anything.\n\n## 3. If it did not ship\n\nNot applicable. For the record, removing it would also require reverting the carrier's admission at `plain-data?`, `machine-data?` and `scalar?`, which is the core of 8.5.5, so REMOVE is not a coherent option.\n\nPlan mode was active, so I wrote no findings file (the brief is read-only anyway); this response is the deliverable.\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,90p' docs/design/datom.world.md; nl -ba src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc | sed -n '355,445p'; sed -n '305,350p' test/yang/python/antlr/float_address_test.cljc; rg -n -A75 '8.5.5' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md | tail -90\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\\n---\\n\\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\\n\\n## Core Philosophy\\n\\nEverything is data.\\nInterpretation > abstraction.\\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\\nRestrictions are a feature.\\n\\n### Foundational Axioms\\n\\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: All IO and data flow through append-only streams.\\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\\n   At system scale, a \\\"system\\\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\\n\\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\\n\\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\\n\\n## Tuples and Datoms\\n\\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\\n\\n## Streams\\n\\nAll IO is modeled as a stream. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\\nNo direct function-to-function coupling without a stream boundary.\\nSide effects must appear as stream emissions.\\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\\n\\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\\nthe stream's own.\\n\\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Agent behavior is specified entirely through stream effects.\\n\\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\\n\\n### Host Boundaries\\n\\nA host boundary is a stream boundary, never a function call. The one\\nsanctioned function-shaped contact is a `dao.stream` handle operation, whose\\nexhaustive outcome map is stream protocol, not an application callback.\\n\\n**Effects out.** Portable code appends an effect value describing what must\\nhappen. A host interpreter consumes that stream, performs the operation, and\\nappends the outcome. Portable code reads the outcome. Correlation is by\\nidentity in the data; neither side holds a reference to the other. A host with\\nno implementation for an effect emits a qualified unsupported result. That is a\\ncorrect outcome, not a gap to be filled.\\n\\n**Events in.** When the host initiates (a socket receives, a connection drops,\\na timer fires) the adapter appends one plain-data event and returns. It invokes\\nnothing. An interpreter reads that stream and decides what the event means.\\nConstructively: **a callback is replaced by a function that transforms an event\\nto data on a stream.** The host's demand for a callback stops at that function.\\n\\n**Adapters.** An adapter is a map of those transform functions. That is all an\\nadapter is. It is a value, not a namespace, and it is general: sockets, files,\\ntimers and windows all deliver events by calling something, and an adapter is\\nwhat they call. Each entry takes the host's arguments and returns one value;\\nthe append is the adapter's, not the entry's, so an entry cannot return a value\\n   355\\t     :yin.k/host-state host-state}))\\n   356\\t\\n   357\\t\\n   358\\t(defn primitive-function\\n   359\\t  \\\"Return the executable value published by a primitive registry entry.\\n   360\\t\\n   361\\t   Bare entries remain supported for composition-supplied legacy registries.\\\"\\n   362\\t  [entry]\\n   363\\t  (if (and (map? entry) (contains? entry :yin.k/function))\\n   364\\t    (:yin.k/function entry)\\n   365\\t    entry))\\n   366\\t\\n   367\\t\\n   368\\t(defn- primitive-entry\\n   369\\t  [name function class arities effects]\\n   370\\t  (assoc (primitive-profile name class arities effects :none)\\n   371\\t         :yin.k/function function))\\n   372\\t\\n   373\\t\\n   374\\t(def ^:private carrier-refusing\\n   375\\t  \\\"Arithmetic and ordering primitives that refuse Jing's float64 carrier on\\n   376\\t   JS. JS would coerce the carrier through its text and answer a string or\\n   377\\t   a wrong number; unwrapping it here would recreate the 1 vs 1.0\\n   378\\t   divergence inside generic yin. Only a profile with an explicit seam\\n   379\\t   (Python's `data/float-value`) computes on floats portably. `=` `==` and\\n   380\\t   `!=` stay host `=` by contract.\\\"\\n   381\\t  '#{+ - * / < > <= >=})\\n   382\\t\\n   383\\t\\n   384\\t(defn- refuse-carrier\\n   385\\t  \\\"`f`, primitive `op`, refusing any float64 carrier argument on JS with a\\n   386\\t   `:yin.k/non-portable` error naming the primitive and the kind; `f`\\n   387\\t   itself on every other host, where float64 content is a host double.\\\"\\n   388\\t  #_{:clj-kondo/ignore [:unused-binding]}\\n   389\\t  [op f]\\n   390\\t  #?(:cljs (letfn [(check\\n   391\\t                     [x]\\n   392\\t                     (when (and (cbor/float64? x) (not (number? x)))\\n   393\\t                       (throw (ex-info (str \\\"Primitive \\\" op\\n   394\\t                                            \\\" refuses a float64 carrier\\\")\\n   395\\t                                       {:yin.k/status :yin.k/non-portable,\\n   396\\t                                        :yin.k/kind :float64-carrier,\\n   397\\t                                        :yin.k/name op}))))]\\n   398\\t             (fn\\n   399\\t               ([] (f))\\n   400\\t               ([a] (check a) (f a))\\n   401\\t               ([a b] (check a) (check b) (f a b))\\n   402\\t               ([a b & more]\\n   403\\t                (check a) (check b) (run! check more)\\n   404\\t                (apply f a b more))))\\n   405\\t     :default f))\\n   406\\t\\n   407\\t\\n   408\\t(def primitives\\n   409\\t  \\\"The standard executable primitive registry, published with UCF §7.5.2\\n   410\\t   profiles. `:yin.k/function` is host-local; the five profile keys are the\\n   411\\t   portable publication. `:variadic` means the greatest listed fixed arity\\n   412\\t   extends to further arguments.\\\"\\n   413\\t  (let [pure (fn [name function arities]\\n   414\\t               [name (primitive-entry name\\n   415\\t                                      (if (contains? carrier-refusing name)\\n   416\\t                                        (refuse-carrier name function)\\n   417\\t                                        function)\\n   418\\t                                      :pure arities #{})])]\\n   419\\t    (into {}\\n   420\\t          [(pure '+ + [0 1 2 :variadic])\\n   421\\t           (pure '- - [1 2 :variadic])\\n   422\\t           (pure '* * [0 1 2 :variadic])\\n   423\\t           (pure '/ checked-divide [1 2 :variadic])\\n   424\\t           (pure '= = [0 1 2 :variadic])\\n   425\\t           (pure '== = [0 1 2 :variadic])\\n   426\\t           (pure '!= not= [1 2 :variadic])\\n   427\\t           (pure '< < [1 2 :variadic])\\n   428\\t           (pure '> > [1 2 :variadic])\\n   429\\t           (pure '<= <= [1 2 :variadic])\\n   430\\t           (pure '>= >= [1 2 :variadic])\\n   431\\t           (pure 'not not [1])\\n   432\\t           (pure 'nil? nil? [1])\\n   433\\t           (pure 'empty? empty? [1])\\n   434\\t           (pure 'first first [1])\\n   435\\t           (pure 'rest (fn [a] (vec (rest a))) [1])\\n   436\\t           (pure 'conj conj [1 2 :variadic])\\n   437\\t           (pure 'assoc assoc [3 :variadic])\\n   438\\t           (pure 'get get [2 3])\\n   439\\t           (pure 'vec vec [1])\\n   440\\t           (pure 'bytes->str\\n   441\\t                 #?(:clj (fn\\n   442\\t                           ([b] (String. ^bytes b \\\"UTF-8\\\"))\\n   443\\t                           ([b enc] (String. ^bytes b ^String enc)))\\n   444\\t                    :cljs (fn [b] (.apply js/String.fromCharCode nil b))\\n   445\\t                    :cljd (fn [b] (dart:core/String.fromCharCodes b)))\\n    (testing \\\"A as projected\\\"\\n      (doseq [[k out] (outputs (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast a))]\\n        (is (= {:py/out [printed], :py/exception nil} out) (str k))))\\n    (testing \\\"A decoded from its canonical bytes: the float64 content the\\n              decoder hands back crosses into arithmetic through\\n              data/float-value\\\"\\n      (doseq [[k out] (outputs (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast\\n                                 (cbor/decode (jing/canonical-bytes a))))]\\n        (is (= {:py/out [printed], :py/exception nil} out) (str k))))))\\n\\n\\n(deftest generic-yin-refuses-the-carrier-test\\n  (testing \\\"generic yin has no float seam: on JS, + over a float64 carrier\\n            decoded from a row refuses, rather than coercing it through its\\n            text into a string; on the JVM and Dart the content is a host\\n            double and + computes\\\"\\n    (let [ast (vm/semantic-bytecode->ast\\n                (cbor/decode\\n                  (jing/canonical-bytes\\n                    (vm/ast->semantic-bytecode\\n                      (u/mark-tails (u/app (u/v '+) (u/lit (cbor/float64 2.0))\\n                                           (u/lit 1)))))))]\\n      (doseq [[k run] runners]\\n        (let [r (try (run ast)\\n                     (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) e\\n                       [:thrown (ex-message e) (ex-data e)]))]\\n          #?(:cljs (is (and (vector? r)\\n                            (= :thrown (first r))\\n                            (re-find #\\\"float64 carrier\\\" (str (second r) (pr-str (nth r 2)))))\\n                       (str k \\\" \\\" (pr-str r)))\\n             :default (is (== 3.0 r) (str k))))))))\\n\\n\\n(deftest dict-keys-test\\n  (testing \\\"1, 1.0 and True key one entry, -0.0 keys as 0, and a float key\\n            is float64 content: the same bytes on every host\\\"\\n    (let [run (fn [form]\\n                (into {}\\n                      (map (fn [[k r]]\\n                             [k (try (r (u/mark-tails\\n                                          (u/then prelude/functions-uast\\n                                                  (u/sexp->uast form))))\\n                                     (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception\\n                                               :cljs :default) e\\n                                       [:thrown (ex-message e)]))]))\\n                      runners))\\n2244-  prelude bodies is unverified, so a test pins this.\\n2245-- No new tag, payload kind, or row shape. Tag 27, signed-zero\\n2246-  preservation, NaN normalization, and the refusals of equal-value,\\n2247-  different-kind collection collisions are unchanged. The generic codec\\n2248-  learns no Python map semantics; `cbor.cljc` and its fixtures are not\\n2249-  edited.\\n2250-\\n2251-Carrier insertion is not a drop-in runtime fix. An execution bridge\\n2252-admits the carrier at all three scalar gates, `plain-data?` and\\n2253-`machine-data?` in `yin.vm` (the row and machine-payload gates, which\\n2254-otherwise refuse to build a carrier-bearing row) and `scalar?` in\\n2255-`yin.vm.engine`, and audits the kind classifier in `yin.vm.values`.\\n2256-The exact-integer carrier is the precedent (section 8.5.4), and\\n--\\n2631:- The float64 address gate of section 8.5.5. Carrier preservation and\\n2632-  the execution bridge complete first; affected rows, derived images,\\n2633-  manifests, and references are then rebuilt. The linked migration\\n2634-  changes bundled roots separately, and neither transition aliases old\\n2635-  integer-misclassified content to corrected float content.\\n2636-\\n2637-The float fix is also a hard prerequisite for P2: the prelude manifest\\n2638-must have one address on every host.\\n2639-\\n2640-The REPL frontend catalog (ruling 16). `yin/repl.cljc` today has a\\n2641-closed `case` over three languages, language special cases, and static\\n2642-requires of all three frontends; its `:python` is the legacy\\n2643-`yang.python`, and the ANTLR parser exists only on the JVM. The design\\n2644-realizes sections 3.3 and 3.6 for the REPL:\\n2645-\\n2646-- `yang.frontend`, new and pure cljc: manifest validation,\\n2647-  `install catalog manifest binding => catalog'`, and selection by\\n2648-  `[id revision]`. The catalog is a value; a manifest carrying a\\n2649-  function or handle is rejected.\\n2650-- The REPL takes the catalog from its composition as a `create-state`\\n2651-  option; each host's `main` builds it and `yin.repl` requires no\\n2652-  frontend. A session pins an immutable catalog snapshot, and each\\n2653-  request pins the selected revision and installed binding. Shell\\n2654-  commands stay the shell's own syntax.\\n2655-- The installed binding is a pair of stages over supplied streams, parse\\n2656-  and lower, plus an optional completeness probe; the REPL steps them to\\n2657-  an outcome. Language-specific parsing and completeness logic stay out\\n2658-  of REPL core. An explicit parser service, worker or remote, has the\\n2659-  same shape; a slow one needs a pending-compile state like the existing\\n2660-  pending-require.\\n2661-- No fallback between frontends: `:yang.python/antlr` and\\n2662-  `:yang.python/legacy` are distinct ids, and legacy retires at its own\\n2663-  migration gate. Selecting a frontend answers unavailable-parser only\\n2664-  when neither a local parser nor an explicitly configured service is\\n2665-  available, on any host (section 4.1).\\n2666-- Support claims derive from explicit semantic and runtime declarations\\n2667-  and tests, not solely from the syntactic `unsupported-rules` list.\\n2668-- A second language (JavaScript, parked) installs as parser artifacts,\\n2669-  a lowering namespace, a published prelude module, a manifest, and one\\n2670-  `install` call in the composition, with no edit to `yin.repl`, Yang\\n2671-  core, or the evaluator.\\n2672-\\n2673-Python's frontend profile pins:\\n2674-\\n2675-```text\\n2676-+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n2677-| Part      | Pin                                                              |\\n2678-+===========+==================================================================+\\n2679-| Grammar   | `grammar-id`, entry rules, export profile `:yang.cst/v1`         |\\n2680-+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n2681-| Lowering  | An immutable implementation revision tied to source or           |\\n2682-|           | build-artifact digests, its dependencies and options, and a      |\\n2683-|           | content-addressed lowering-profile descriptor (ruling 17). A     |\\n2684-|           | golden-corpus digest is supplementary evidence, not identity;    |\\n2685-|           | host code needs no executable UAST to have artifact identity.    |\\n2686-+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n2687-| Runtime   | `py` and `pysp` manifest addresses, the safepoint profile map,   |\\n2688-|           | host-module profile addresses (cell, data, integer with limits,  |\\n2689-|           | stream poll), and permitted effects                              |\\n2690-+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n2691-| Support   | Explicit declarations and tests (ruling 16); reference runtime   |\\n2692-|           | CPython 3.9.6                                                    |\\n2693-+-----------+------------------------------------------------------------------+\\n2694-```\\n2695-\\n2696-The REPL `__main__` persists across submissions (ruling 18): its module\\n2697-object, globals dict, builtin namespace, and import cache. `(reset)` is a\\n2698-new Python process: fresh task-owned runtime state, handler stacks and\\n2699-hook attachments included, while immutable verified code may be reused.\\n2700-Runtime state never merges across frontend revisions, and late results\\n2701-from the old process cannot mutate the new one.\\n2702-\\n2703-Foreign-principal imports are not claimed until D7 slice B lands with\\n2704-its tests (ruling 19), verifying closure origin and store ownership\\n2705-recursively through exports, re-exports, and transitive dependency\\n2706-slices before any executable value is exposed. B is necessary, not\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.started\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '2220,2315p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md; rg -n 'host.*arithmetic|host.*equality|primitive.*host|host.*primitive' docs/design/yin.vm*.md | head -20\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"\",\"exit_code\":null,\"status\":\"in_progress\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '2220,2315p' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/docs/design/yang.antlr.md; rg -n 'host.*arithmetic|host.*equality|primitive.*host|host.*primitive' docs/design/yin.vm*.md | head -20\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"> representation. A Python float wrapper alone does not establish the\\n> numeric kind of its payload. Row projection and transformation must\\n> preserve that kind.\\n\\nThe mechanism is Jing's existing `dao.jing/float64` carrier (tag 27),\\ninserted at the producer, under these constraints:\\n\\n- One representation. Every `:py/float` payload is Jing float64 content,\\n  `cbor/float64` of the value: the identity on JVM and Dart, the carrier\\n  on JavaScript. On JavaScript every payload is a carrier, non-integral\\n  values included, since a carrier equals only another carrier.\\n- Kind is fixed where it is still known. Lowering constructs the carrier\\n  while literal syntax still identifies a float; the prelude marks its\\n  float constants explicitly before JavaScript collapses them. A later\\n  scan of bare numbers cannot recover the distinction.\\n- The prelude seam. `py/float` wraps and `py/num` unwraps through two\\n  new pure functions in the `data` module's table (section 9.3);\\n  arithmetic between them stays host-native on bare numbers. The quoted\\n  prelude carries no integral float literal: `(* 1.0 x)` coercions and\\n  constants such as `1.0` and `0.0` go through the data functions, while\\n  a non-integral constant such as `0.5` stays. A JVM test fails on any\\n  integral-valued double in a prelude literal row.\\n- No capture inside the seam: no yield or safepoint sits between an\\n  unwrap and its rewrap. Whether the safepoint stage marks sites inside\\n  prelude bodies is unverified, so a test pins this.\\n- No new tag, payload kind, or row shape. Tag 27, signed-zero\\n  preservation, NaN normalization, and the refusals of equal-value,\\n  different-kind collection collisions are unchanged. The generic codec\\n  learns no Python map semantics; `cbor.cljc` and its fixtures are not\\n  edited.\\n\\nCarrier insertion is not a drop-in runtime fix. An execution bridge\\nadmits the carrier at all three scalar gates, `plain-data?` and\\n`machine-data?` in `yin.vm` (the row and machine-payload gates, which\\notherwise refuse to build a carrier-bearing row) and `scalar?` in\\n`yin.vm.engine`, and audits the kind classifier in `yin.vm.values`.\\nThe exact-integer carrier is the precedent (section 8.5.4), and\\n`yin.vm.debruijn` already sees through the float carrier. Values keep\\ntheir float kind whenever they return to addressed data. Two boundary\\naudits follow: `float-repr` in the renderer unwraps the carrier on\\nJavaScript, and Node test expectations comparing bare numbers move to\\nconstructors or `content=`.\\n\\nOpen: the ruling leaves undecided the numeric kind of the float that\\nC1's dict-key normalization places in a map key. C3 ruling 6 replaces\\nthat normalization with reduced-rational keys (section 8.5.4, slice\\nS5); until then the C1 key needs a decision.\\n\\nWith identical float bits and hash algorithm, corrected rows have\\nidentical bytes and addresses on every host. Migration:\\n\\n```text\\n+----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\\n| Artifact                         | Effect                                    |\\n+==================================+===========================================+\\n| Python literal rows, JVM and     | Bytes unchanged                           |\\n| Dart                             |                                           |\\n+----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\\n| Python literal rows, Node        | Re-minted to match JVM and Dart           |\\n+----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\\n| Prelude rows                     | Change on every host, so every bundled    |\\n|                                  | unit's address changes; a prelude-profile |\\n|                                  | bump (section 11)                         |\\n+----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\\n| Parent rows, A and A' roots,     | Re-minted wherever a changed row is       |\\n| address-bearing references       | reachable                                 |\\n+----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\\n| Jing canonical fixtures          | Unchanged                                 |\\n+----------------------------------+-------------------------------------------+\\n```\\n\\nAffected artifacts are rebuilt from source. An integer address is never\\naliased to a float address.\\n\\n\\\"Float-free\\\" describes the entire addressed payload, including the\\nbundled prelude, not merely the user's Python source. Since the prelude\\nitself carries the divergent literals, no bundled unit is float-free\\ntoday. Host-specific goldens do not establish portability. Until the\\npreparatory slice lands, none of the following is allowed:\\n\\n- a cross-host pin of a bundled-unit root, A or A', even for a\\n  float-free user program;\\n- a cross-host pin of a snapshot or continuation holding a runtime\\n  float;\\n- a Node-specific golden for such trees;\\n- a C2-S5 cross-host golden on a float-bearing tree;\\n- an edit to `cbor.cljc` or its fixtures.\\n\\nThe gate on float-bearing address acceptance releases only when tests\\non JVM, Node, and Dart establish identical canonical bytes and A and A'\\nroots for float-bearing inputs and the full prelude; preservation\\nthrough projection and decoding; distinct integer and float\\nidentities; signed-zero behavior; and working execution across the\\nrequired evaluators.\\n\\nWhat may proceed meanwhile:\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.register.md:748:A normal `:call` whose resolved host primitive returns a\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.dht.md:1569:The primitive behind it is a host library, never hand-rolled: `java.security`\\ndocs/design/yin.vm-portability.md:227:If a user needs a highly specific, optimized piece of host logic (e.g., direct DOM manipulation in JS, or hardware registers in C), they define a primitive using Yang's `define-primitive` macro.\\ndocs/design/yin.vm-portability.md:246:When `ast-datoms->host-source` processes the stream, it intercepts these `:yin.macro/host-code` datoms and directly stamps their string values into the host skeleton's `@@(primitives)@@` block. \\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:18:2. **Recursive Portable Encoding:** Section 1.1 permits host functions and local stream handles in the environment. A recursive encoding over a **disjoint tagged grammar** is required — one a program's own map literals cannot counterfeit — to encode these local references into portable descriptors, together with per-primitive **semantic profiles** (a name recovers a binding, not a meaning) and explicit, total failure modes for un-serializable resources. Cursor aliasing is observable and must survive the round trip.\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:57:host functions, and nothing says which primitives it assumed.\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:870:- **`:host`-class functions are refused** (kind `:host-state-primitive`). A\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:989:`:host-object | :unnamed-function | :ambiguous-primitive | :host-state-primitive\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:995:host resources behind stream descriptors and named, profiled primitives,\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md:1769:  Add a host object, unnamed or ambiguous function, host-state primitive,\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.targets.md:120:foreign host will be a few hundred lines, as Ribbit's is; the primitive\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.targets.md:581:same size as a Direction B host's value model and primitives, because it\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.debruijn.targets.md:1118:8. T-D8 primitives: foreign hosts implement the pure primitive table\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.linker.md:1819:    function and implements no cryptographic primitive itself (a host\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.semantic.md:282:`:primitives`, which is how the walker already avoids putting host objects in\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.macro.md:521:(an unresolved name, a prelude rejection, a host primitive failure). The\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.dependency-completion.md:73:     :refusals    [{:kind :ambiguous-primitive | :unnamed-function | :host-state-primitive\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.dependency-completion.md:110:      host function object → [:primitive sym]     ; by reverse lookup, §6; ambiguity refuses\\ndocs/design/yin.vm.dependency-completion.md:242:`:unnamed-function`; `:host` class → `:host-state-primitive` (all UCF §7.5.2).\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"agent_message\",\"text\":\"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-03 03:36:25 GMT\\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-03 10:36:25 Asia/Ho_Chi_Minh\\n\\n**REMOVE — remove the generic primitive refusal wrappers; retain the float-address fix, scalar admission, executable-image fixes, and explicit Python conversion seam.**\\n\\n**1. The refusal exceeds this slice’s contract.**\\n\\nMy earlier acceptance of a Python-specific execution seam did **not** authorize changing the standard arithmetic primitives. Section 8.5.5 places conversion in the prelude’s `data/float-value` / `data/float64` seam and explicitly keeps arithmetic between them host-native.\\n\\nThe new [carrier-refusing definition](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-py-floatfix/src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:374) instead changes eight standard primitives according to the carrier’s physical host representation. The same addressed operand computes on JVM/Dart and receives a newly specified refusal on JavaScript. That is not a portable numeric contract.\\n\\nThe motivation—preventing accidental JavaScript coercion—is valid, but the remedy needs a separate generic-numeric ruling. Removing this guard does **not** establish that generic arithmetic over decoded carriers is supported or portable. That remains outside this slice’s acceptance claim.\\n\\nThe architectural separation is:\\n\\n- Jing owns canonical numeric content and identity.\\n- VM scalar gates admit and preserve that content.\\n- Numeric consumers supply arithmetic semantics.\\n- Python’s prelude supplies its explicit conversion seam.\\n\\nNo profile detection, new representation, or persisted “came from a carrier” flag belongs in generic evaluator dispatch.\\n\\n**2. Neither JS-only refusal nor uniform refusal should be added here.**\\n\\nA uniform carrier refusal cannot distinguish a wrapped float from an ordinary host double on JVM/Dart: the constructor is the identity there. Refusing all doubles would also reject the native arithmetic values deliberately produced by Python’s seam. Adding provenance tags to distinguish them would contradict the adopted representation and persist information unnecessary to numeric meaning.\\n\\nRecord the scope in **both places**, with different responsibilities:\\n\\n- **§8.5.5:** “This slice leaves standard Yin arithmetic and equality primitives unchanged. Python converts float64 content explicitly for arithmetic and restores float64 content before values return to addressed data. Generic arithmetic over decoded float64 carriers is not established by this gate.”\\n- **UCF scalar-arm paragraph:** “Admission and round-trip preservation of a numeric scalar do not imply that every primitive accepts it; execution requires the consumer’s declared numeric contract.”\\n\\nDo not record JS-specific arithmetic refusal as UCF non-portability. The value is serializable; the issue is the arithmetic consumer.\\n\\nThe existing `+`-only test would be insufficient **if these wrappers were retained**: all eight operations, operand positions, and supported unary/variadic forms would require coverage, with exact outcome-data assertions. Under this ruling, remove that refusal test instead of expanding it.\\n\\n**3. Exact implementation disposition**\\n\\nRemove:\\n\\n- `carrier-refusing`;\\n- `refuse-carrier`;\\n- their wrapping inside the standard primitive registry;\\n- `generic-yin-refuses-the-carrier-test` and claims that this refusal is architectural behavior.\\n\\nRestore the prior primitive bindings, including `/`’s existing `checked-divide`. Do not replace them with automatic carrier unwrapping, and do not alter `=`, `==`, or `!=`.\\n\\nRetain:\\n\\n- producer-side float64 construction;\\n- Python’s explicit unwrap/rewrap seam;\\n- carrier-aware row, machine, engine, and value-classification gates;\\n- de Bruijn scalar admission and faithful IEEE-bit extraction, including `−0.0`;\\n- canonical byte/address and decoded-Python execution tests.\\n\\nAdd or retain a generic **literal pass-through and re-encoding** test proving that the VM preserves carrier content without interpreting it arithmetically. Keep Python arithmetic tests across all four evaluators and three hosts.\\n\\nThis preserves one canonical representation and host-stable addresses without silently expanding the standard primitive contract. A universal carrier-aware numeric tower remains a separate design task.\\n\\nRead-only inspection; no files changed or tests run.\"}}\n{\"type\":\"turn.completed\",\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":4584367,\"cached_input_tokens\":3846272,\"cache_write_input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":20469,\"reasoning_output_tokens\":1441}}\n   315\t               (and (= (byte-at a i) (byte-at b i))\n   316\t                    (recur (inc i))))))))\n   317\t\n   318\t\n   319\t;; ==========================================================================\n   320\t;; Numeric kinds and carriers\n   321\t;; ==========================================================================\n   322\t\n   323\t#?(:cljs\n   324\t   (defn- neg-zero?\n   325\t     [x]\n   326\t     (and (zero? x) (neg? (/ 1 x)))))\n   327\t\n   328\t\n   329\t#?(:cljs\n   330\t   (defn- double-bits-hex\n   331\t     \"The 16 hex digits of a JS number's IEEE-754 bits.\"\n   332\t     [d]\n   333\t     (let [view (js/DataView. (js/ArrayBuffer. 8))]\n   334\t       (.setFloat64 view 0 d)\n   335\t       (apply str (map #(let [b (.getUint8 view %)]\n   336\t                          (str (when (< b 16) \"0\") (.toString b 16)))\n   337\t                       (range 8))))))\n   338\t\n   339\t\n   340\t#?(:cljs\n   341\t   (deftype Float64\n   342\t     [v]\n   343\t\n   344\t     Object\n   345\t\n   346\t     (toString [_] (str v))\n   347\t\n   348\t\n   349\t     IEquiv\n   350\t\n   351\t     (-equiv\n   352\t       [_ other]\n   353\t       (and (instance? Float64 other)\n   354\t            (or (and (js/isNaN v) (js/isNaN (.-v ^Float64 other)))\n   355\t                (js/Object.is v (.-v ^Float64 other)))))\n   356\t\n   357\t\n   358\t     IHash\n   359\t\n   360\t     (-hash [_] (hash (if (js/isNaN v) \"nan\" (double-bits-hex v))))\n   361\t\n   362\t\n   363\t     IPrintWithWriter\n   364\t\n   365\t     (-pr-writer\n   366\t       [_ writer _]\n   367\t       (-write writer (str \"#dao.jing/float64 \" (pr-str v))))))\n   368\t\n   369\t\n   370\t(deftype Rational\n   371\t  [numerator denominator]\n   372\t  #?@(:cljd [cljd.core/IEquiv\n   373\t             (-equiv [_ other]\n   374\t                     (and (instance? Rational other)\n   375\t                          (= numerator (.-numerator ^Rational other))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:334:       (.setFloat64 view 0 d)\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:341:   (deftype Float64\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:353:       (and (instance? Float64 other)\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:354:            (or (and (js/isNaN v) (js/isNaN (.-v ^Float64 other)))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:355:                (js/Object.is v (.-v ^Float64 other)))))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:444:   (defmethod print-method Rational\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:462:   Float64 carrier or a non-integral, non-finite or negative-zero number\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:467:     :cljs (or (instance? Float64 x)\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:496:   the JVM, the Float64 carrier on JavaScript (so integral floats such as\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:501:     :cljs (Float64. (if (instance? Float64 x) (.-v x) (js/Number x)))))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:524:             (.getFloat64 bd 0))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:529:             (Float64. (.getFloat64 view 0)))))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:537:     :cljs (if (instance? Float64 x) (.-v x) x)))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:631:             (.setFloat64 bd 0 d)\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:642:             (.setFloat64 view 0 d)\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:986:                        (.setFloat64 bd 0 f))\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:998:                        (.setFloat64 view 0 f))\ndocs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:164:addresses anything printable/readable** — characters, `#inst`, `#uuid`, and\ndocs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:204:  Never parse or print these components to reconstruct an identifier;\ndocs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:353:(`+ - * / quot rem mod inc dec abs`) remain host-native and reject carrier\ndocs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:567:the superseded transitional-printer limitations. Name exactly which\ndocs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:570:pathological print collisions, ambient print-var leakage), byte-array\ndocs/design/dao.jing.cbor.md:620:  independence from ambient print settings.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
